BE-300 2.4.18: make sys_pipe() actually return the second descriptor.

MIPS sys_pipe() takes the saved user registers by value
(`struct pt_regs regs`, which is the pt_regs frame on the kernel stack)
and returns the write end of the pipe by storing it in regs.regs[3]
(user v1).  To a modern compiler that is a store to a by-value parameter
that is never read again, so gcc 10 deletes it: the compiled sys_pipe()
only returns fd[0] in v0, and userspace reads whatever happened to be in
v1 as the write end.

Every pipe() on the source-rebuilt 2.4.18 kernel therefore got a bogus
second descriptor.  Shell pipelines lost their writer, $(...) printed a
garbled error and hung the shell, and any Qt/OPIE code using pipes broke.
Store through a volatile pointer so the write to the pt_regs frame is
kept.

--- a/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -47,7 +47,12 @@
 		res = error;
 		goto out;
 	}
-	regs.regs[3] = fd[1];
+	/*
+	 * regs is the caller's pt_regs frame passed by value; a plain store
+	 * to it is dead to the compiler and gcc >= 4 drops it, losing the
+	 * second descriptor.
+	 */
+	((volatile struct pt_regs *)&regs)->regs[3] = fd[1];
 	res = fd[0];
 out:
 	return res;
